🔥  𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗨𝗽, 𝗕𝘂𝘁 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗔𝗰𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes Service exists, but users cannot access the application. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I wouldn't immediately restart the pods or recreate the Service.

First, I would trace the request path.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check the Service

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>

Verify the Service type, port and target port.

2️⃣ Check Service details

kubectl describe svc <service-name> -n <namespace>

Look for selector and endpoint-related information.

3️⃣ Check Endpoints

kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

If there are no endpoints, I would investigate whether the Service selector matches the pod labels.

4️⃣ Check Pod labels

kubectl get pods --show-labels -n <namespace>

Compare the pod labels with the Service selector.

5️⃣ Test the application inside the cluster

kubectl run test-pod --rm -it --image=curlimages/curl -- sh

Then test the Service:

curl http://<service-name>:<port>

This helps determine whether the problem is with the application/Service or external access.

6️⃣ Check the exposure layer

If the Service is exposed externally, I would check:

🔹 LoadBalancer
🔹 Ingress
🔹 Ingress Controller
🔹 DNS
🔹 Network policies
🔹 Cloud load balancer configuration

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Client → DNS → Ingress/LB → Service → Endpoints → Pod → Application

At every layer, I ask:

“Where exactly is the request failing?”

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A Kubernetes Service being Running or existing successfully doesn't guarantee that traffic can reach the application.

Always trace the complete request path before making changes. 🚀
